The rediscovery battery (TESTING.md §8).

The methodology's own acceptance test, and the one seance is unusually equipped
to pass: the defect catalogue already exists. The August 2026 migration produced
real failures with known mechanisms, and the harness must rediscover them when
their protections are reverted.

Each row of table.tsv names a patch that removes one protection and the test
that must then fail. run.sh applies each patch to a SCRATCH COPY of the tree,
runs the named test there, and requires a failure. The working tree is never
touched, so there is no restore step and nothing to forget to undo.

    sh tests/rediscovery/run.sh --tier 6

--tier is required, never defaulted: tier 6 needs root, ZFS and vnet jails, and
takes minutes per row. Nothing runs this automatically. It is the check you run
before you believe the suite, not the one you run after every edit.

Run it from inside the reaper guest, and give it the same environment `reaper
run` gives the suite. The pseudo-cluster refuses to guess where it may write
(D-22), so an ssh session that has not inherited reaper's environment must say
so itself:

    env REAPER_STATE=/tank/state sh tests/rediscovery/run.sh --tier 6

Without it every row fails with "neither SEANCE_CLUSTER_BASE nor REAPER_STATE
is set" — correctly, and confusingly, since the row that could not run reads
much like the row that ran and found nothing. run.sh distinguishes them (it
refuses to call a run with zero assertions a rediscovery), which is what makes
the mistake recoverable rather than a false pass.

The rows, and what each is about:

  recv-no-x-mountpoint.patch
      Removes -x mountpoint from the receive. 'zfs send -p' carries a
      dataset's locally set properties, a CBSD guest's dataset carries a local
      mountpoint, and a receive without the flag plants it on the replica: a
      second dataset claiming the live guest's mount. The August defect,
      verbatim.

      What catches it is NOT the property assertion -- seance also holds the
      law on every tick and would repair the property before the assertion
      looked. What catches it is the assertion that a clean tick has nothing to
      repair. Defence in depth means one reverted layer is caught by the layer
      that notices it working.

  quorum-rule-removed.patch
      Makes rung 2 act whatever the arithmetic said. The quorum rule is what
      stops an ISOLATED node -- one that is perfectly alive and can reach
      nobody -- from concluding that everybody else has died and promoting
      their estates on top of them. TESTING.md §8 names `concurrency` for this
      row; it is pointed at `quorum` instead, because that is the stage where
      the rule is load-bearing. In `concurrency` two heirs are told about one
      real death and the thing that stops the second one is CARP and the claim
      check, not quorum -- both survivors form a majority there, so reverting
      the rule changes nothing and a row pointing at it would be a row that can
      never fail. In `quorum` the isolated node freezes for this reason and no
      other, and reverting it produces the two actors the methodology asks for.

  debounce-removed.patch
      Makes rung 1 report a pass without waiting and without re-reading CARP.
      The debounce is what tells a death from a link that flapped: a transition
      that was true when devd fired and is not true `debounce` seconds later is
      a node that never died. Caught by the `flap` stage, which heals the link
      inside the window -- with the wait removed the ladder runs straight past
      the healing node, fences it, and promotes an estate that was never
      orphaned.

  verifier-masks-crash.patch
      Makes `lineage_probe` swallow the listing's stderr and read an empty
      listing that exited 0 as "the newest replica is now". The August
      catalogue's crashed verifier: a checker that died, said nothing, and
      whose silence was taken for a pass. Caught at tier 4 by
      t_ladder_lineage.sh's empty0 rows and by the ladder table's
      `lineage-empty0` row.

      IT WAS NOT CAUGHT BY THE LADDER TABLE UNTIL THIS PATCH WAS WRITTEN. The
      `lineage=empty0` world scripted the mock with a key built from a
      TWO-LINE variable; the script came out malformed, the mock answered a
      contract error, and the row passed while measuring something else
      entirely. The fixture now empties the listing seam directly. A row that
      stays green when its protection is reverted is a finding about the row,
      and this is what one looks like.

  promotion-without-fencing.patch (tier 6 stage `quorum`, tier 7)
      Beyond the `promote` stage it already had: in `quorum` the target is
      ISOLATED rather than dead, so a promotion that did not fence leaves the
      guest running in two places -- which is the invariant the product exists
      for, rather than a missing record. At tier 7 the same revert is invariant
      2 (a promotion with no evidence) and invariant 1 under an isolation.

  the tier-7 rows
      Four rows drive tests/tier7/t_sim.sh with a protection reverted, per
      DESIGN.md §10. They are run with

          env SEANCE_SIM_SEEDS=2950315648 SEANCE_SIM_STEPS=27 \
              SEANCE_SIM_NO_SHRINK=1 sh tests/rediscovery/run.sh --tier 7

      because a tier-7 row asserts that a deliberately reverted protection is
      NOTICED: noticing it once is the whole answer, and the default battery's
      five seeds would be four more cluster rebuilds for the same bit. The
      shrinker is off for the same reason -- it exists to reduce a trace nobody
      understands, and here the cause is the patch that was just applied.
      Neither switch changes what is asserted: the run still has to fail.

      THERE IS NO TIER-7 ROW FOR recv-no-x-mountpoint, and DESIGN.md §10
      predicted one ("invariant 4a under hand-mount"). It was written, run, and
      removed: seance re-holds the shadow-mount law on EVERY tick (D-65), so by
      the time the driver observes properties the replica has already been
      repaired and invariant 4a has nothing to see. Measured, not reasoned --
      the row ran 25 of its 27 steps with the receive flag removed and nothing
      fired. What catches that revert is the tier-6 `repl` row, which asserts
      that a CLEAN tick repairs nothing; a tier-7 row that cannot fail would
      read as coverage of a protection nothing was checking (D-125's rule,
      applied to a row of this project's own making).

      THE SEED AND THE STEP COUNT ARE PART OF THE ROW, and choosing them badly
      is how a tier-7 row comes to prove nothing. A reverted protection is only
      noticed by a trace that reaches it: the first window tried here
      (1043489862 x 23) contained one `promote` and it froze at rung 2 for want
      of a quorum, so `promotion without fencing` reverted a rung the trace
      never walked and the row passed. 2950315648 was chosen by reading its
      trace: `kill alpha` at 1, a promotion that COMPLETES at 4 (two nodes
      alive, so quorum forms), an isolation at 12, a hand-mount at 22 and --
      the one the boot gate needs -- `return alpha` at 26, after its estate has
      moved. A row whose window does not contain the event its protection
      guards is a row that cannot fail, which is worse than no row (D-125).

  repl-skip-resume-token.patch
      Makes the engine ignore the peer's receive_resume_token. A dataset
      holding a token refuses every other stream -- "destination contains
      partially-complete state from zfs receive -s" -- so a fat guest whose
      send was interrupted would never replicate again, while looking busy
      every tick. Caught by the interrupt stage, which kills a send mid-stream
      and then requires the next tick to finish it.

Adding a row: write the patch against the current tree with

    diff -u a/<file> b/<file> > tests/rediscovery/<name>.patch

(paths relative, applied with patch -p1 at the repository root), add the row to
table.tsv, and run the battery. A patch that no longer applies is itself a
finding -- the protection it reverts has moved, and run.sh says so rather than
passing over it.
